{"id": "5b365ff2-3ad3-4aeb-849f-708fb6b78777", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T09:29:29.125998", "source": "environment", "parent_id": "21c77270-1668-4a5f-ba0e-6796973fbfaf", "tool_name": "execute_check", "tool_call_id": "call-5c2abc1f-057d-4fb1-82d0-413dd71b5ff6", "observation": {"content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"phase\": \"exploration\", \"command\": \"ls -la /workspace/ && echo \\\"---\\\" && cat /workspace/task.json && echo \\\"---\\\" && uname -a && echo \\\"---\\\" && nvidia-smi 2>&1 | head -5 && echo \\\"---\\\" && curl -s -m 2 https://www.google.com -o /dev/null -w \\\"network: %{http_code}\\\\n\\\" 2>&1 || true\", \"exit_code\": 0, \"timed_out\": false, \"elapsed_seconds\": 0.032, \"output\": \"total 8\\ndrwx------  2 1776737281 748400513 4096 Oct 21 16:28 .\\ndrwxr-xr-x 12 1776737281 748400513  240 Oct 21 16:29 ..\\n-rw-------  1 1776737281 748400513  445 Oct 21 16:28 task.json\\n---\\n{\\n  \\\"question\\\": \\\"Do the optimized instructions discovered by OPRO outperform the baseline instructions ('Let's think step by step.' and the empty prompt) on BBH tasks?\\\",\\n  \\\"context\\\": \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\",\\n  \\\"documents\\\": {\\n    \\\"track.md\\\": \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\\n  }\\n}\\n---\\nLinux d33eaef-lcedt 7.0.0-38-generic #38~24.04.4-Ubuntu SMP PREEMPT_DYNAMIC Mon Sep 14 16:37:11 UTC 2 x86_64 x86_64 x86_64 GNU/Linux\\n---\\nNVIDIA-SMI has failed because it couldn't communicate with the NVIDIA driver. Make sure that the latest NVIDIA driver is installed and running.\\n\\n---\\nnetwork: 000\\n\", \"output_truncated\": false}"}], "is_error": false, "kind": "BenchmarkObservation"}, "action_id": "21c77270-1668-4a5f-ba0e-6796973fbfaf", "extended_content": [], "kind": "ObservationEvent", "source_event_index": 15}
{"id": "5eaf84fb-d541-4de7-b4f0-c221ae5b288d", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T09:30:20.444486", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "5b365ff2-3ad3-4aeb-849f-708fb6b78777", "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-06d201e9-0964-4d36-b62a-4fdf505f8fa4", "tool_call": {"id": "call-06d201e9-0964-4d36-b62a-4fdf505f8fa4", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\":\"Initial inspection and design sketch (plan stage)\",\"stage\":\"plan\",\"answer\":\"## (1) Input / Repository Inspection\\n\\nThe repository at `/workspace` contains exactly one file:\\n\\n- `/workspace/task.json` (7 lines, 445 bytes). It carries only:\\n  - `question`: \\\"Do the optimized instructions discovered by OPRO outperform the baseline instructions ('Let's think step by step.' and the empty prompt) on BBH tasks?\\\"\\n  - `context`: \\\"Design an experiment that answers the question. The researcher can clarify source-supported setup details by topic. Do not claim that an experiment was run.\\\"\\n  - `documents.track.md`: literally the string `\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` (a pointer, not an attached document).\\n- No `README`, no source code, no scripts, no config files, no data files, no manifests, no pre-existing OPRO artifacts, no pre-existing BBH artifacts. The grandparent (`/`) has no project tree either; `~` (HOME) is empty.\\n\\nEnvironment observations (relevant because execution would differ from design here):\\n- Linux bubblewrap, system Python only, **no GPU** (`nvidia-smi` fails), **network disabled** (`curl` returns `000`), 24 CPUs.\\n- No model weights, no API credentials, and no dataset are preinstalled. `task.json` explicitly notes `datasets: []` and that project dependencies are not preinstalled.\\n\\nBecause the supplied materials are empty of artifacts, the experiment design below necessarily relies on **publicly available OPRO and BBH materials** (Yang et al. 2023 \\\"Large Language Models as Optimizers\\\"; Suzgun et al. 2022 \\\"Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them\\\"). I will mark every dependency on those materials as an assumption and flag every place where the source intent is genuinely unknown.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (2) Proposed Approach (design sketch)\\n\\nTreat this as a **three-condition, within-subjects** evaluation on a fixed set of BBH examples. Each example is scored under each of the three instruction conditions; accuracies are compared per task and aggregated.\\n\\n### a) BBH subset\\n- **Default proposal:** the 23-task BBH benchmark from Suzgun et al. (2022) `{boolean_expressions, causal_judgement, date_understanding, disambiguation_qa, dyck_languages, formal_fallacies, geometric_shapes, hyperbaton, logical_deduction_five_objects, logical_deduction_seven_objects, logical_deduction_three_objects, movie_recommendation, multistep_arithmetic_two, navigate, object_counting, penguins_in_a_table, reasoning_about_colored_objects, ruin_names, salient_translation_error_detection, snarks, sports_understanding, temporal_sequences, tracking_shuffled_objects_three_objects, tracking_shuffled_objects_five_objects, tracking_shuffled_objects_seven_objects, web_of_lies, word_sorting}` — that's 27, so please treat this list as illustrative of the canonical set pending confirmation (see flag in §3).\\n- **Refinement matching the OPRO setup:** Yang et al. (2023) restrict to the subset whose answers are single strings (excluding tasks whose answers are sets/lists, e.g. `penguins_in_a_table` and `geometric_shapes`) → **21 tasks**. I will **default to those 21 tasks**, with the explicit exclusion list as a design parameter.\\n- **Eval split:** the 250-example per-task evaluation split released with BBH (`lukaemon/bbh` on HuggingFace, or the original `suzgunmirac/BIG-Bench-Hard` repo), so the comparison is on identical items across conditions.\\n\\n### b) OPRO-discovered instructions: sourcing\\n- **Default:** **reuse the per-task optimized instructions published in Yang et al. (2023), Appendix** (one instruction per BBH task). This is the only choice that is actually feasible inside this bubblewrap environment (no network, no model API, no weights). These instructions are public and citable.\\n- **Optional secondary condition (only if researcher wants a wire-up test):** a \\\"general/cross-task\\\" OPRO instruction (single best instruction over all tasks), as reported in the same paper. Treat as a secondary cell.\\n- **Re-running OPRO from scratch is out of scope**: it requires thousands of model calls against an instruction-tuned LLM. I will mark this option as \\\"available only with an external compute/API environment\\\" rather than pretend to plan it inside the current sandbox.\\n\\n### c) Model(s)\\n- **Default (matches the OPRO paper claim about BBH):** instruction-tuned **PaLM 2-L** (and, in parallel, **text-bavinci-002** / **gpt-3.5-turbo** if a comparison across model families is desired).\\n- **Open-weights fallback** if the researcher wants a runnable setup: an instruction-tuned model loadable via local weights (e.g., `Llama-3.x-Instruct`, `Mistral-7B-Instruct`). Whichever model is chosen will be **fixed across all three conditions** to make the comparison fair.\\n- The current environment has no weights and no API access, so any concrete model choice is an **assumption to be confirmed**.\\n\\n### d) Decoding setup\\n- **Greedy decoding** (temperature 0.0, top_p 1.0, max_new_tokens sufficient to fit a chain-of-thought), per-sample, no batching effects.\\n- Single deterministic pass per (task × example × instruction condition). Greedy makes seed averaging unnecessary; if a non-deterministic model is used, plan for **N=3 random seeds** with identical prompts and report mean ± stdev, then take the median seed for statistical tests. Default: deterministic pass.\\n\\n### e) Baselines (the two cells named in the question)\\n1. **Empty prompt** (`\\\"\\\"`): no instruction prefix; only the raw BBH example (question + few-shot exemplars if any).\\n2. **\\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"** (Kojima et al. 2022): appended/prepended exactly as the canonical zero-shot CoT trigger.\\n3. **OPRO instruction** (per-task, from Yang et al. 2023 Appendix).\\n\\n(Optionally a 4th cell: \\\"general OPRO instruction\\\"; mentioned above as secondary.)\\n\\n### f) Prompt-construction protocol\\nFor each BBH task and each example:\\n1. **Instruction prefix** = condition-specific string (empty / \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\" / OPRO-optimized).\\n2. **Few-shot exemplars** = **zero-shot by default**, to match the OPRO paper's headline comparison. If 3-shot CoT is desired instead, hold it constant across all three conditions.\\n3. **Question** = the BBH example verbatim.\\n4. **Answer extraction** = first occurrence of the model's final answer (e.g., a single letter, \\\"yes\\\"/\\\"no\\\", or the literal answer string), after stripping chain-of-thought and any \\\"the answer is …\\\" prefix. Each task supplies a small, fixed extractor (regex or string normalize).\\n\\nThis protocol is **identical across the three conditions** except for the instruction prefix, so any accuracy difference is attributable to the instruction.\\n\\n### g) Scoring metric(s)\\n- **Primary:** exact-match (EM) accuracy after canonical answer normalization (lower-case, trim punctuation, collapse whitespace).\\n- **Per-task special cases** (with explicit, scripted extractors, not LLM-as-judge):\\n  - yes/no tasks → normalize to `{yes,no}`.\\n  - multiple-choice → match the option letter (A/B/C/…) when present, else the option text.\\n  - numeric tasks → numeric tolerance where BBH's own evaluator uses tolerance (e.g. `object_counting` exact integer, `multistep_arithmetic_two` exact integer).\\n- **No LLM-as-judge** for BBH, because BBH already provides ground-truth answers suitable for EM.\\n\\n### h) Number of trials / seeds\\n- Each (task × condition) is run on the **full 250-example eval split** — that is the unit of statistical comparison.\\n- Because decoding is greedy (or median of 3 deterministic seeds), \\\"trials\\\" in the classical sense collapse to one deterministic score per cell.\\n- **Per-task sample size = 250**; **n_tasks = 21**; this gives reasonable power for paired non-parametric tests at the task level.\\n\\n### i) Statistical comparison plan\\n- **Cell-level metric:** per-task accuracy `acc(task, condition)`.\\n- **Macro accuracy:** unweighted mean of `acc` over the 21 tasks per condition.\\n- **Primary test:** paired tests over the 21 task accuracies, since every condition sees the same items.\\n  - **Wilcoxon signed-rank test** (non-parametric, robust to small n=21) for each pairwise contrast: `{OPRO vs empty}`, `{OPRO vs. \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"}`, `{empty vs. \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"}` for completeness.\\n  - **Bonferroni-corrected α = 0.05 / 3 ≈ 0.017** to control family-wise error across the three contrasts.\\n- **Secondary test:** paired **bootstrap** of per-example accuracy deltas (10,000 resamples) clustered by task, to give a 95% CI on the macro difference. Reported alongside, not replacing, the Wilcoxon test.\\n- **Effect size:** Cliff's δ or matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation at the task level; macro accuracy difference `Δ = acc(OPRO) − acc(baseline)` in absolute % points.\\n- **Multiple comparisons across BBH subsets:** if a second BBH subset is added (e.g., the full 23 tasks instead of 21), apply Bonferroni per task family.\\n- **Report both raw and corrected p-values**, and pre-register which contrast is primary (OPRO vs. each baseline) before unblinding.\\n\\n### j) Fairness / leakage controls\\n- Identical prompts modulo the instruction prefix.\\n- Identical answer extractors across conditions.\\n- Identical eval split per task.\\n- No use of test labels during prompt construction (OPRO instructions are taken from the published paper; they were not fit on the BBH eval items).\\n- If the model's tokenizer was exposed to the BBH eval items during pre-training, this is acknowledged but not corrected for (matches OPRO paper's protocol).\\n\\n### k) Deliverables that the design commits to producing\\n1. A 21 × 3 table of per-task accuracies (tasks × {empty, \\\"step-by-step\\\", OPRO}).\\n2. Macro averages with 95% bootstrap CIs.\\n3. Per-task accuracy bars (or heatmap).\\n4. Wilcoxon p-values plus Bonferroni-corrected p-values, effect sizes, and macro deltas.\\n5. A short qualitative error analysis: which task families (numeric, multi-choice, yes/no, symbolic) gain the most from OPRO.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Consequential Assumptions and Uncertainties (need researcher confirmation before locking the design)\\n\\nThese are choices I am making **because the supplied repository carries nothing**. Items marked \\\"**flag**\\\" are places where the source intent is genuinely unknown and should be decided before the design is finalized.\\n\\n### A. Assumptions (stated explicitly, even if conventional)\\n\\n1. The reference OPRO paper is **Yang et al., 2023 (\\\"Large Language Models as Optimizers\\\")**. Its Appendix is the source of the per-task optimized instructions.\\n2. The reference BBH definition is **Suzgun et al., 2022, 23 tasks** (some sources say 27; we will pin to the 23-task canonical set as the version used by the OPRO paper).\\n3. The optimized instruction is the **per-task instruction** published in Yang et al. (2023), not a single \\\"universal\\\" OPRO instruction, unless the researcher wants the universal instruction as an extra cell.\\n4. Greedy decoding at temperature 0.0 (OPRO's protocol) is acceptable for the head-to-head comparison, because the question is about instructions, not about sampling variance.\\n5. Eval split = the 250-item per-task subset released in BBH; train splits (`few-shot`) are not used to build prompts in the zero-shot default cell.\\n6. Scoring = exact match after the per-task answer-normalization rules built into BBH's reference evaluator — we will **not** introduce LLM-as-judge.\\n7. Statistical comparison uses paired Wilcoxon on 21 task accuracies with Bonferroni correction, plus a paired bootstrap CI on macro deltas.\\n8. Re-running OPRO optimization inside this sandbox is **not** feasible (network disabled, no API, no weights) and is therefore **not** part of the design.\\n\\n### B. Uncertainties to resolve before locking the design (flagged)\\n\\n1. **Which OPRO-author instruction set should the experiment reuse?** The paper publishes distinct per-task optimized instructions and at least one \\\"general\\\" instruction. → **flag**: choose `per-task` vs. `universal`.\\n2. **Which exact BBH subset?** Suzgun's 23 tasks vs. Yang's 21 tasks vs. the broader BIG-Bench Hard list of 27. Different subsets can move the macro number by several points. → **flag**.\\n3. **Which model?** PaLM 2-L (OPRO's primary), GPT-class API model (`text-davinci-002` / `gpt-3.5-turbo`), or open-weights (`Llama-3.x-Instruct`, `Mistral-7B-Instruct`). Each changes feasibility and reproducibility. → **flag**.\\n4. **Is network/API access available for any execution at all?** Currently `network: disabled`; any \\\"run\\\" of this experiment would require either an offline open-weights model or an out-of-sandbox API. → **flag**.\\n5. **Few-shot or zero-shot?** OPRO's headline comparison on BBH is **zero-shot**, but some BBH results report 3-shot CoT. Without fixing this, baseline comparisons are not apples-to-apples. → **flag**.\\n6. **Should the answer-style hint (e.g., \\\"Answer with one of: yes, no\\\") be appended to the empty-prompt baseline?** The OPRO paper sometimes binds this hint to the instruction prefix. If the empty prompt is `\\\"\\\"`, do we still include the style hint? → **flag**, because the answer affects whether \\\"empty\\\" beats or loses to the CoT trigger.\\n7. **Number of seeds** under any non-deterministic decoder. If the chosen model cannot do greedy deterministically (e.g., hosted APIs that still inject temperature), N seeds need to be specified. → **flag**.\\n8. **The release/source of BBH data.** `lukaemon/bbh` differs from the original Suzgun files in item ordering; should we match the version used in Yang et al. 2023 for direct comparability, or the current canonical release? → **flag**.\\n9. **Acceptable α and correction strategy.** I proposed Wilcoxon + Bonferroni; the researcher may prefer Holm or FDR. → **flag**.\\n10. **Inclusion of a \\\"neutral paraphrase\\\" baseline** (e.g., a hand-written, plausible-sounding but non-optimized instruction) as a control for instruction-quality-vs.-OPRO-specific effects. → **flag**.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Bottom line for the researcher:** the repo contains nothing beyond the task spec, so the design above leans entirely on publicly available OPRO/BBH artifacts and asks for your confirmation on ten flagged points before I lock down the per-task list, instruction source, model, and statistical plan. Once those are settled, I will hand back the full design with feasibility checks, controls, and repository observations spelled out as evidence.\",\"evidence\":\"Direct repository inspection: `/workspace` lists only `task.json` (7 lines, 445 bytes) whose `documents.track.md` is the literal string `\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` No source code, configs, scripts, READMEs, manifests, OPRO artifacts, or BBH artifacts are present. Directory tree `find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` returned only that file; `~` is empty; no project directory exists under `/`. Environment inspection: `nvidia-smi` reports no NVIDIA driver (no GPU); `curl -m 2 https://www.google.com` returns HTTP 000 (network disabled); system Python on Linux, 24 CPUs, no project dependencies installed. `task.json` itself states `datasets: []`. Therefore the experiment design depends on **publicly available OPRO/BBH materials** (Yang et al., 2023; Suzgun et al., 2022), and every dependency on those materials is marked as an assumption; no successful exploratory command constitutes a completed experiment because the task is explicitly design-only.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"The current bubblewrap sandbox cannot host model execution; any concrete run of this experiment requires either offline open-weights models or an out-of-sandbox API.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"Re-running OPRO optimization inside this sandbox is not feasible (network off, no API, no weights) and is therefore not part of this design.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Statistical comparison is paired Wilcoxon signed-rank over 21 task accuracies with Bonferroni-corrected α (≈0.017 across 3 contrasts), supplemented by a paired bootstrap 95% CI on macro-deltas and Cliff's δ / matched-pairs rank-biserial effect sizes.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Scoring is EM after per-task answer normalization taken from the BBH reference evaluator; no LLM-as-judge.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Greedy decoding (temperature 0.0, top_p 1.0) is used to match OPRO's protocol and remove sampling variance as a confound.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Eval split uses the 250-example per-task BBH evaluation subset, identical across the three instruction conditions.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"The reference BBH definition is Suzgun et al., 2022, 23 tasks; OPRO narrows this to 21 by excluding tasks whose answers are not single strings (e.g., penguins_in_a_table, geometric_shapes).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"The reference OPRO paper is Yang et al., 2023 (\\\"Large Language Models as Optimizers\\\"); per-task optimized instructions are sourced from its Appendix.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Whether to add a \\\"neutral paraphrase\\\" baseline to control for instruction-quality vs. OPRO-specific effects.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"Preferred multiple-comparison correction (Bonferroni vs. Holm vs. FDR) and α.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Source/version of BBH data files (lukaemon/bbh vs. original Suzgun release vs. Yang et al.'s frozen split).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Number of decoder seeds (0/1/3) given partial non-determinism of hosted models.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the answer-style hint is bound to the instruction prefix (so the empty-prompt cell has no hint) or always appended.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Zero-shot vs. 3-shot (or other few-shot) exemplar regime, kept constant across conditions.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether any execution environment with network/API/weights is available, or whether the deliverable must remain strictly design-only.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Which model family to evaluate on (PaLM 2-L vs. GPT-class API vs. open-weights instruction-tuned model).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether to use Suzgun's 23 tasks, Yang's 21 tasks, or the broader 27-task BIG-Bench Hard set.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Which OPRO instruction set is the target of the question (per-task optimized vs. universal/cross-task optimized instruction).</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-03844c4b-ddf5-4b6d-8abc-230ba5f28a8d", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 16}
