d in parallel **text-davinci-002 / gpt-3.5-turbo** if cross-family comparison is desired.\\n- **Open-weights fallback** if the researcher wants a runnable setup: an instruction-tuned model loadable via local weights (e.g., Llama-3.x-Instruct, Mistral-7B-Instruct). Whichever model is chosen is **fixed across all three conditions** to keep the comparison fair.\\n- The current environment has no weights and no API access, so any concrete model choice is an **assumption to be confirmed**.\\n\\n### d) Decoding setup\\n- **Greedy decoding** (temperature 0.0, top_p 1.0, max_new_tokens sufficient to fit a chain-of-thought), per-sample.\\n- Single deterministic pass per (task × example × instruction condition). Greedy makes seed averaging unnecessary; if a non-deterministic model is used, plan for **N=3 random seeds** with identical prompts and report mean ± stdev, then take the median seed for statistical tests. Default: one deterministic pass.\\n\\n### e) Baselines (the two cells named in the question)\\n1. **Empty prompt** (`\\\"\\\"`): no instruction prefix; only the raw BBH example (question + few-shot exemplars if any).\\n2. **\\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"** (Kojima et al. 2022): appended/prepended exactly as the canonical zero-shot CoT trigger.\\n3. **OPRO instruction** (per-task, from Yang et al. 2023 Appendix).\\n\\n(Optional 4th cell: \\\"general OPRO instruction\\\" — secondary.)\\n\\n### f) Prompt-construction protocol\\nFor each BBH task and each example:\\n1. **Instruction prefix** = condition-specific string (empty / \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\" / OPRO-optimized).\\n2. **Few-shot exemplars** = **zero-shot by default**, to match the OPRO paper's headline comparison. If 3-shot CoT is desired instead, hold it constant across all three conditions.\\n3. **Question** = the BBH example verbatim.\\n4. **Answer extraction** = first occurrence of the model's final answer (e.g., a single letter, \\\"yes\\\"/\\\"no\\\", or the literal answer string), after stripping chain-of-thought and any \\\"the answer is …\\\" prefix. Each task supplies a small, fixed extractor (regex or string normalize).\\n\\nThis protocol is **identical across the three conditions** except for the instruction prefix, so any accuracy difference is attributable to the instruction.\\n\\n### g) Scoring metric(s)\\n- **Primary:** exact-match (EM) accuracy after canonical answer normalization (lowercase, trim punctuation, collapse whitespace).\\n- **Per-task special cases** (explicit, scripted extractors — not LLM-as-judge):\\n  - yes/no tasks → normalize to `{yes, no}`.\\n  - multiple-choice → match the option letter (A/B/C/…) when present, else the option text.\\n  - numeric tasks → exact integer (e.g. `object_counting`, `multistep_arithmetic_two`); no float tolerance by default.\\n- **No LLM-as-judge** for BBH; BBH already has ground-truth answers suitable for EM.\\n\\n### h) Number of trials / seeds\\n- Each (task × condition) is run on the **full 250-example eval split** — that is the unit of statistical comparison.\\n- Because decoding is greedy (or median of 3 deterministic seeds), \\\"trials\\\" collapse to one deterministic score per cell.\\n- **Per-task sample size = 250**; **n_tasks = 21**; reasonable power for paired non-parametric tests at the task level.\\n\\n### i) Statistical comparison plan\\n- **Cell-level metric:** per-task accuracy `acc(task, condition)`.\\n- **Macro accuracy:** unweighted mean of `acc` over the 21 tasks per condition.\\n- **Primary test:** paired tests over the 21 task accuracies, since every condition sees the same items.\\n  - **Wilcoxon signed-rank test** (non-parametric, robust to small n=21) for each pairwise contrast: `{OPRO vs empty}`, `{OPRO vs \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"}`, `{empty vs \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"}` for completeness.\\n  - **Bonferroni-corrected α = 0.05 / 3 ≈ 0.017** to control family-wise error across the three contrasts.\\n- **Secondary test:** paired **bootstrap** of per-example accuracy deltas (10,000 resamples) clustered by task, to give a 95% CI on the macro difference. Reported alongside, not replacing, the Wilcoxon test.\\n- **Effect size:** Cliff's δ or matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation at the task level; macro accuracy difference `Δ = acc(OPRO) − acc(baseline)` in absolute % points.\\n- **Multiple comparisons across BBH subsets:** if a second BBH subset is added (e.g., the full 23 tasks instead of 21), apply Bonferroni per task family.\\n- **Pre-registration:** specify which contrast is primary (OPRO vs. each baseline) before unblinding.\\n\\n### j) Fairness / leakage controls\\n- Identical prompts modulo the instruction prefix.\\n- Identical answer extractors across conditions.\\n- Identical eval split per task.\\n- No use of test labels during prompt construction; OPRO instructions come from the published paper (not fit on the BBH eval items).\\n- Acknowledge (but do not attempt to correct for) the possibility that the LLM's pretraining corpus contained BBH eval items — this matches OPRO's protocol.\\n\\n### k) Deliverables that the design commits to producing\\n1. A 21 × 3 table of per-task accuracies (tasks × {empty, \\\"step-by-step\\\", OPRO}).\\n2. Macro averages with 95% bootstrap CIs.\\n3. Visual: per-task accuracy bars or a heatmap.\\n4. Wilcoxon p-values (raw + Bonferroni-corrected), effect sizes, macro deltas.\\n5. A short qualitative error analysis: which task families (numeric, multi-choice, yes/no, symbolic) gain the most from OPRO.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## (3) Consequential Assumptions and Uncertainties\\n\\n### A. Assumptions (stated explicitly, even if conventional)\\n\\n- The reference OPRO paper is **Yang et al., 2023 (\\\"Large Language Models as Optimizers\\\")**. Its Appendix is the source of the per-task optimized instructions.\\n- The reference BBH definition is **Suzgun et al., 2022, 23 tasks**, narrowed to OPRO's **21-task** subset (excluding tasks whose answers are not single strings).\\n- The OPRO instruction used is the **per-task instruction** published in Yang et al. (2023), not a single \\\"universal\\\" OPRO instruction, unless the researcher wants the universal instruction as an extra cell.\\n- Greedy decoding at temperature 0.0 (OPRO's protocol) is acceptable for the head-to-head comparison, because the question is about instructions, not sampling variance.\\n- Eval split = the 250-item per-task BBH evaluation subset; train splits are not used to build prompts in the zero-shot default cell.\\n- Scoring = exact match after the per-task answer-normalization rules built into BBH's reference evaluator; **no LLM-as-judge**.\\n- Statistical comparison = paired Wilcoxon on 21 task accuracies with Bonferroni correction across three contrasts, plus a paired bootstrap CI on macro deltas.\\n- Re-running OPRO optimization inside this sandbox is **not** feasible (network disabled, no API, no weights) and therefore is **not** part of the design.\\n\\n### B. Uncertainties to resolve before locking the design\\n\\n1. **Which OPRO-author instruction set should the experiment reuse?** Per-task optimized vs. universal/cross-task optimized. Both are reported by Yang et al. (2023).\\n2. **Which exact BBH subset?** Suzgun's 23 tasks vs. Yang's 21 tasks vs. the broader 27-task BIG-Bench Hard list. Different subsets can move the macro number by several points.\\n3. **Which model?** PaLM 2-L (OPRO's primary), GPT-class API model (`text-davinci-002` / `gpt-3.5-turbo`), or open-weights (`Llama-3.x-Instruct`, `Mistral-7B-Instruct`). Each changes feasibility and reproducibility.\\n4. **Is network/API access available for any execution at all?** Currently `network: disabled`; any \\\"run\\\" of this experiment would require either an offline open-weights model or an out-of-sandbox API.\\n5. **Few-shot or zero-shot?** OPRO's headline comparison on BBH is zero-shot, but some BBH results elsewhere use 3-shot CoT. Fixing this is required for an apples-to-apples baseline.\\n6. **Answer-style hint policy.** Should the answer-style hint (e.g., \\\"Answer with one of: yes, no\\\") be appended to the empty-prompt baseline or bound only to non-empty instructions? This affects whether \\\"empty\\\" beats or loses to the CoT trigger.\\n7. **Number of seeds** under any non-deterministic decoder (relevant for hosted APIs that don't expose greedy cleanly).\\n8. **Release/source of BBH data.** `lukaemon/bbh` differs slightly from the original Suzgun files in item ordering; match the version used in Yang et al. 2023 for direct comparability, or use the current canonical release.\\n9. **Multiple-comparison correction strategy.** Bonferroni vs. Holm vs. FDR, and α.\\n10. **Neutral-paraphrase baseline.** Whether to add a hand-written, plausible non-optimized instruction as a control for instruction-quality effects independent of OPRO.\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Bottom line for the researcher:** the repo contains nothing beyond the task spec, so the design above leans entirely on publicly available OPRO/BBH artifacts and asks for your confirmation on ten flagged points before I lock down the per-task list, instruction source, model, and statistical plan. Once those are settled, I will hand back the full design with feasibility checks, controls, and repository observations spelled out as evidence.\",\"evidence\":\"Direct repository inspection: `/workspace` lists only `task.json` (7 lines, 445 bytes), whose `documents.track.md` is the literal string `\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` No source code, configs, scripts, READMEs, manifests, OPRO artifacts, or BBH artifacts are present. `find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f` returned only that file; `~` is empty; no project directory exists under `/`. Environment inspection: `nvidia-smi` reports no NVIDIA driver (no GPU); `curl -m 2 https://www.google.com` returns HTTP 000 (network disabled); system Python on Linux, 24 CPUs, no project dependencies installed. `task.json` itself states `datasets: []`. Therefore the experiment design depends on **publicly available OPRO/BBH materials** (Yang et al., 2023; Suzgun et al., 2022), and every dependency on those materials is marked as an assumption; no successful exploratory command constitutes a completed experiment because the task is explicitly design-only.\",\"assumptions\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"The reference OPRO paper is Yang et al., 2023 (\\\"Large Language Models as Optimizers\\\"); per-task optimized instructions are sourced from its Appendix.</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"The reference BBH definition is Suzgun et al., 2022, 23 tasks; OPRO narrows this to 21 by excluding tasks whose answers are not single strings (e.g., penguins_in_a_table, geometric_shapes).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Eval split uses the 250-example per-task BBH evaluation subset, identical across the three instruction conditions.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Greedy decoding (temperature 0.0, top_p 1.0) is used to match OPRO's protocol and remove sampling variance as a confound.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Scoring is EM after per-task answer normalization taken from the BBH reference evaluator; no LLM-as-judge.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Statistical comparison is paired Wilcoxon signed-rank over 21 task accuracies with Bonferroni-corrected alpha (about 0.017 across 3 contrasts), supplemented by a paired bootstrap 95% CI on macro-deltas and Cliff's delta / matched-pairs rank-biserial effect sizes.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Re-running OPRO optimization inside this sandbox is not feasible (network off, no API, no weights) and is therefore not part of this design.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"The current bubblewrap sandbox cannot host model execution; any concrete run of this experiment requires either offline open-weights models or an out-of-sandbox API.</item>\\n\"}],\"uncertainties\":[{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":{\"item\":\"Which OPRO instruction set is the target of the question (per-task optimized vs. universal/cross-task optimized instruction).</item>\\n\",\"$text\":\"Whether to use Suzgun's 23 tasks, Yang's 21 tasks, or the broader 27-task BIG-Bench Hard set.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Which model family to evaluate on (PaLM 2-L vs. GPT-class API vs. open-weights instruction-tuned model).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether any execution environment with network/API/weights is available, or whether the deliverable must remain strictly design-only.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Zero-shot vs. 3-shot (or other few-shot) exemplar regime, kept constant across conditions.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether the answer-style hint is bound to the instruction prefix (so the empty-prompt cell has no hint) or always appended.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Number of decoder seeds (0/1/3) given partial non-determinism of hosted models.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Source/version of BBH data files (lukaemon/bbh vs. original Suzgun release vs. Yang et al.'s frozen split).</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Preferred multiple-comparison correction (Bonferroni vs. Holm vs. FDR) and alpha.</item>\\n\"},\"$text\":\"Whether to add a 'neutral paraphrase' baseline to control for instruction-quality vs. OPRO-specific effects.</item>\\n\"}]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-1f59cce8-8964-4ea6-9dcd-c4e1366d00ea", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 18}
