e, no scripts, no config files, no data files, no manifests, no pre-existing OPRO artifacts, no pre-existing BBH artifacts. The grandparent (/) has no project tree either; ~ (HOME) is empty. find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f returned only that single file.\\n\\nEnvironment observations that constrain any execution:\\n- Linux bubblewrap, system Python only, no GPU (nvidia-smi reports no driver), network disabled (curl returns 000), 24 CPUs.\\n- No model weights, no API credentials, no dataset preinstalled. task.json states datasets: [] and notes project dependencies are not preinstalled.\\n\\nBecause the supplied materials are empty of artifacts, the design below necessarily relies on publicly available OPRO and BBH materials (Yang et al. 2023 \\\"Large Language Models as Optimizers\\\"; Suzgun et al. 2022 \\\"Challenging BIG-Bench Tasks and Whether Chain-of-Thought Can Solve Them\\\"). Every dependency on those materials is marked as an assumption, and every place where the source intent is genuinely unknown is flagged.\\n\\n## (2) Proposed Approach (design sketch)\\n\\nTreat this as a three-condition, within-subjects evaluation on a fixed set of BBH examples. Each example is scored under each of the three instruction conditions; accuracies are compared per task and aggregated.\\n\\na) BBH subset\\n- Default proposal: canonical BBH from Suzgun et al. 2022. The OPRO paper restricts to a 21-task subset by removing tasks whose answers are sets or multiple strings (e.g., penguins_in_a_table, geometric_shapes). I will default to the 21-task subset.\\n- Eval split: the 250-example per-task evaluation split released with BBH (lukaemon/bbh on HuggingFace, or the original suzgunmirac/BIG-Bench-Hard repo), so the comparison is on identical items across conditions.\\n\\nb) OPRO-discovered instructions: sourcing\\n- Default: reuse the per-task optimized instructions published in Yang et al. 2023, Appendix (one instruction per BBH task). This is the only sourcing choice actually feasible inside this bubblewrap environment (no network, no model API, no weights). These instructions are public and citable.\\n- Optional secondary condition: a \\\"general/cross-task\\\" OPRO instruction (single best instruction across all tasks), as reported in the same paper. Treated as a secondary cell.\\n- Re-running OPRO from scratch is out of scope: it requires thousands of model calls against an instruction-tuned LLM. It is \\\"available only in an external compute/API environment\\\", not planned inside the sandbox.\\n\\nc) Model(s)\\n- Default (matches the OPRO paper claims about BBH): instruction-tuned PaLM 2-L, and in parallel text-davinci-002 / gpt-3.5-turbo if cross-family comparison is desired.\\n- Open-weights fallback if the researcher wants a runnable setup: an instruction-tuned model loadable via local weights (e.g., Llama-3.x-Instruct, Mistral-7B-Instruct). Whichever model is chosen is fixed across all three conditions so the comparison stays fair.\\n- The current environment has no weights and no API access, so any concrete model choice is an assumption to be confirmed.\\n\\nd) Decoding setup\\n- Greedy decoding (temperature 0.0, top_p 1.0, max_new_tokens sufficient to fit a chain-of-thought), per-sample.\\n- Single deterministic pass per (task x example x instruction condition). Greedy makes seed averaging unnecessary; if a non-deterministic model is used, plan for N=3 random seeds with identical prompts and report mean +/- stdev, then take the median seed for statistical tests. Default: one deterministic pass.\\n\\ne) Baselines (the two cells named in the question)\\n1. Empty prompt (\\\"\\\"): no instruction prefix; only the raw BBH example (question + few-shot exemplars if any).\\n2. \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\" (Kojima et al. 2022): appended/prepended exactly as the canonical zero-shot CoT trigger.\\n3. OPRO instruction (per-task, from Yang et al. 2023 Appendix).\\n\\nOptional 4th cell: \\\"general OPRO instruction\\\", secondary.\\n\\nf) Prompt-construction protocol\\nFor each BBH task and each example:\\n1. Instruction prefix = condition-specific string (empty / \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\" / OPRO-optimized).\\n2. Few-shot exemplars = zero-shot by default, to match the OPRO paper's headline comparison. If 3-shot CoT is desired instead, hold it constant across all three conditions.\\n3. Question = the BBH example verbatim.\\n4. Answer extraction = first occurrence of the model's final answer (a single letter, yes/no, or the literal answer string), after stripping chain-of-thought and any \\\"the answer is ...\\\" prefix. Each task supplies a small, fixed extractor (regex or string normalize).\\n\\nThis protocol is identical across the three conditions except for the instruction prefix, so any accuracy difference is attributable to the instruction.\\n\\ng) Scoring metric(s)\\n- Primary: exact-match (EM) accuracy after canonical answer normalization (lowercase, trim punctuation, collapse whitespace).\\n- Per-task special cases (explicit, scripted extractors, not LLM-as-judge):\\n  - yes/no tasks: normalize to {yes, no}.\\n  - multiple-choice: match the option letter (A/B/C/...) when present, else the option text.\\n  - numeric tasks: exact integer (e.g., object_counting, multistep_arithmetic_two); no float tolerance by default.\\n- No LLM-as-judge for BBH; BBH already has ground-truth answers suitable for EM.\\n\\nh) Number of trials / seeds\\n- Each (task x condition) is run on the full 250-example eval split; that is the unit of statistical comparison.\\n- Because decoding is greedy (or median of 3 deterministic seeds), \\\"trials\\\" collapse to one deterministic score per cell.\\n- Per-task sample size = 250; n_tasks = 21; reasonable power for paired non-parametric tests at the task level.\\n\\ni) Statistical comparison plan\\n- Cell-level metric: per-task accuracy acc(task, condition).\\n- Macro accuracy: unweighted mean of acc over the 21 tasks per condition.\\n- Primary tests: paired tests over the 21 task accuracies, since every condition sees the same items.\\n  - Wilcoxon signed-rank test (non-parametric, robust to small n=21) for each pairwise contrast: OPRO vs empty, OPRO vs \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\", empty vs \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\" for completeness.\\n  - Bonferroni-corrected alpha = 0.05 / 3 approx 0.017 to control family-wise error across the three contrasts.\\n- Secondary test: paired bootstrap of per-example accuracy deltas (10000 resamples) clustered by task, to give a 95% CI on the macro difference. Reported alongside, not replacing, the Wilcoxon test.\\n- Effect size: Cliff's delta or matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation at the task level; macro accuracy difference Delta = acc(OPRO) - acc(baseline) in absolute % points.\\n- Pre-registration: specify which contrast is primary (OPRO vs each baseline) before unblinding.\\n\\nj) Fairness / leakage controls\\n- Identical prompts modulo the instruction prefix.\\n- Identical answer extractors across conditions.\\n- Identical eval split per task.\\n- No use of test labels during prompt construction; OPRO instructions come from the published paper (not fit on the BBH eval items).\\n- Acknowledge (but do not attempt to correct for) the possibility that the LLM pretraining corpus contained BBH eval items; this matches OPRO's protocol.\\n\\nk) Deliverables that the design commits to producing\\n1. A 21 x 3 table of per-task accuracies (tasks x {empty, step-by-step, OPRO}).\\n2. Macro averages with 95% bootstrap CIs.\\n3. Visual: per-task accuracy bars or a heatmap.\\n4. Wilcoxon p-values (raw + Bonferroni-corrected), effect sizes, macro deltas.\\n5. A short qualitative error analysis showing which task families (numeric, multi-choice, yes/no, symbolic) gain the most from OPRO.\\n\\n## (3) Consequential Assumptions and Uncertainties\\n\\nA. Assumptions (stated explicitly)\\n- The reference OPRO paper is Yang et al. 2023 (\\\"Large Language Models as Optimizers\\\"); its Appendix is the source of the per-task optimized instructions.\\n- The reference BBH definition is Suzgun et al. 2022, 23 tasks, narrowed to OPRO's 21-task subset (excluding tasks whose answers are not single strings).\\n- The OPRO instruction used is the per-task instruction published in Yang et al. 2023, not a single universal OPRO instruction, unless the researcher requests the universal instruction as an extra cell.\\n- Greedy decoding at temperature 0.0 is acceptable for the head-to-head comparison, because the question is about instructions, not sampling variance.\\n- Eval split = the 250-item per-task BBH evaluation subset; train splits are not used to build prompts in the zero-shot default cell.\\n- Scoring = exact match after the per-task normalization rules built into BBH's reference evaluator; no LLM-as-judge.\\n- Statistical comparison = paired Wilcoxon on 21 task accuracies with Bonferroni correction across three contrasts, plus a paired bootstrap CI on macro deltas.\\n- Re-running OPRO optimization inside this sandbox is not feasible (network disabled, no API, no weights) and is therefore not part of the design.\\n\\nB. Uncertainties to resolve before locking the design\\n1. Which OPRO-author instruction set should the experiment reuse: per-task optimized vs. universal/cross-task optimized.\\n2. Which exact BBH subset: Suzgun's 23 vs. Yang's 21 vs. the broader 27-task BIG-Bench Hard list.\\n3. Which model family: PaLM 2-L (OPRO's primary), GPT-class API, or open-weights instruction-tuned model.\\n4. Whether any execution environment with network/API/weights is available at all, or whether the deliverable must remain strictly design-only.\\n5. Whether the comparison should be zero-shot or 3-shot CoT (kept constant across conditions).\\n6. The answer-style hint policy: whether the hint (e.g., \\\"Answer with one of: yes, no\\\") is bound to the instruction prefix or always appended.\\n7. Number of decoder seeds (1 vs 3) given partial non-determinism of hosted APIs.\\n8. Release/source of BBH data: lukaemon/bbh vs. original Suzgun files vs. Yang et al.'s frozen split.\\n9. Multiple-comparison correction strategy: Bonferroni vs. Holm vs. FDR, and the chosen alpha.\\n10. Whether to add a neutral-paraphrase baseline to control for instruction-quality effects independent of OPRO.\\n\\nBottom line for the researcher: the repo contains nothing beyond the task spec, so the design above leans entirely on publicly available OPRO/BBH artifacts and asks for confirmation on the ten flagged points above before I lock the per-task list, instruction source, model, and statistical plan. Once those are settled, I will hand back the full design with feasibility checks, controls, and repository observations spelled out as evidence.\", \"evidence\": \"Direct repository inspection: /workspace lists only task.json (7 lines, 445 bytes), whose documents.track.md is the literal string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" No source code, configs, scripts, READMEs, manifests, OPRO artifacts, or BBH artifacts are present. find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f returned only that file; ~ is empty; no project directory exists under /. Environment inspection: nvidia-smi reports no NVIDIA driver; curl -m 2 against https://www.google.com returns HTTP 000 (network disabled); system Python on Linux, 24 CPUs, no project dependencies installed. task.json itself states datasets: []. The design therefore depends on publicly available OPRO/BBH materials (Yang et al., 2023; Suzgun et al., 2022), and every dependency on those materials is marked as an assumption. No successful exploratory command constitutes a completed experiment because the task is explicitly design-only. The track.md pointer in task.json literally states \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"\", \"assumptions\": [\"The reference OPRO paper is Yang et al. 2023 Large Language Models as Optimizers; per-task optimized instructions are sourced from its Appendix.\", \"The reference BBH definition is Suzgun et al. 2022 with the OPRO 21-task subset (excluding non-single-string-answer tasks such as penguins_in_a_table and geometric_shapes).\", \"Eval split uses the 250-example per-task BBH evaluation subset, identical across the three instruction conditions.\", \"Greedy decoding at temperature 0.0 with top_p 1.0 matches OPRO's protocol and removes sampling variance as a confound.\", \"Scoring is exact-match after the per-task answer normalization defined in BBH's reference evaluator; no LLM-as-judge is introduced.\", \"Statistical comparison uses paired Wilcoxon signed-rank over 21 task accuracies with Bonferroni-corrected alpha across the three contrasts, supplemented by paired bootstrap 95% CIs and Cliff's delta / matched-pairs rank-biserial effect sizes.\", \"Re-running OPRO optimization inside this sandbox is not feasible (network off, no API, no weights) and is therefore not part of this design.\", \"The current bubblewrap sandbox cannot host model execution; any concrete run of this experiment requires either offline open-weights models or an out-of-sandbox API.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Which OPRO instruction set is the target of the question: per-task optimized vs. universal/cross-task optimized instruction.\", \"Whether to use Suzgun's 23 tasks, Yang's 21 tasks, or the broader 27-task BIG-Bench Hard set.\", \"Which model family to evaluate on: PaLM 2-L vs. GPT-class API vs. an open-weights instruction-tuned model.\", \"Whether any execution environment with network/API/weights is available, or whether the deliverable must remain strictly design-only.\", \"Zero-shot vs. few-shot exemplar regime, kept constant across conditions.\", \"Whether the answer-style hint is bound to the instruction prefix (so the empty-prompt cell has no hint) or always appended.\", \"Number of decoder seeds (1 vs 3) given partial non-determinism of hosted models.\", \"Source/version of BBH data files: lukaemon/bbh vs. original Suzgun release vs. Yang et al.'s frozen split.\", \"Preferred multiple-comparison correction (Bonferroni vs. Holm vs. FDR) and alpha.\", \"Whether to add a neutral-paraphrase baseline to control for instruction-quality vs. OPRO-specific effects.\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-bad8c919-e96d-4ceb-926b-e1998f06ef14", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 20}
