{"id": "a0948825-3aef-468b-b571-a2d909fdc036", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T09:32:12.143709", "source": "user", "parent_id": "ee0a967c-6039-4342-9321-cb3cd65602b6", "llm_message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"cache_prompt": false, "type": "text", "text": "{\"researcher_request\": \"Approach approved with the following resolutions to the flagged uncertainties. With these locked, please produce the FINAL design document (synthesis) covering rationale, population, conditions, controls, materials, procedure, metrics, statistical analysis, pre-registration, feasibility, and limitations. Do not claim any experiment has been run; this is design-only. Treat the locked choices as requirements and the remaining items as design polish within them. The final document should be self-contained so a reviewer can assess it without reading the agent's plan message again.\", \"required_handoff_stage\": \"result\", \"approach_approved\": true}"}], "tool_calls": null, "tool_call_id": null, "name": null}, "llm_response_id": null, "activated_skills": [], "extended_content": [], "sender": null, "kind": "MessageEvent", "source_event_index": 22}
{"id": "44737262-f060-4641-a901-b7e3fab08fe4", "timestamp": "2026-10-21T09:34:14.657868", "source": "agent", "parent_id": "a0948825-3aef-468b-b571-a2d909fdc036", "action": {"stage": "result", "answer": "# Final Design Document — OPRO vs. Baselines on BBH\n\n> **Status:** Design-only. No experiment has been executed in this bubblewrap (no GPU, network disabled, no model/API). The design below is self-contained and runnable in an environment that has access to the chosen model and the BBH data.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Executive Summary\n\nWe will run a within-subjects, three-condition head-to-head evaluation on the 21-task OPRO subset of BIG-Bench Hard (BBH). Each BBH evaluation example is scored under (i) the empty instruction \"\", (ii) \"Let's think step by step.\", and (iii) the per-task OPRO-optimized instruction published in Yang et al. 2023 Appendix. The model under test is instruction-tuned PaLM 2-L; decoding is greedy (T=0.0); scoring is exact-match with per-task normalization. Primary inference is paired Wilcoxon signed-rank over 21 task accuracies with Bonferroni-corrected α = 0.05/3, supplemented by a paired bootstrap 95% CI on macro-deltas. Pre-registered hypotheses: H1 — OPRO > \"\", H2 — OPRO > \"step by step.\", each on macro-accuracy.\n\n## 1. Scientific Question and Pre-Registered Hypotheses\n\n**Research question:** Do the optimized instructions discovered by OPRO outperform the baseline instructions (`\"\"` and `\"Let's think step by step.\"`) on BBH tasks?\n\n**Pre-registered hypotheses** (binary, pre-specified before data inspection):\n\n- **H1:** μ_OPRO > μ_empty on macro BBH accuracy (21-task mean).\n- **H2:** μ_OPRO > μ_step-by-step on macro BBH accuracy.\n- **H0 (control contrast, exploratory):** μ_empty = μ_step-by-step — included to verify the well-known finding that \"step by step\" beats the empty prompt under zero-shot CoT; if H0 is rejected with empty < step-by-step, the baseline ordering of H1/H2 is sensible.\n\n**Decision rule:** A hypothesis is declared supported if its one-sided Wilcoxon p-value (corrected for 2 primary contrasts) is below α = 0.025 (Bonferroni split of 0.05 across H1 and H2) **and** the bootstrap CI on the macro-delta excludes zero.\n\n**Falsification criteria (would refute the headline claim):**\n- H1 not supported (Δ macro ≤ 0, p ≥ α), or\n- H2 not supported (Δ macro ≤ 0, p ≥ α), or\n- Per-task inspection shows OPRO wins only on tasks where the per-task instruction was clearly overfit (e.g., contains task-specific keywords), suggesting leakage.\n\n## 2. Rationale\n\nThe OPRO paper's central empirical claim is that LLM-optimized instruction prefixes exceed hand-crafted zero-shot CoT triggers on BBH. That claim has been reported in one paper with specific models. A rigorous confirmation requires: (a) identical prompts modulo the instruction prefix, (b) a paired per-item evaluation so task-level differences cancel, (c) per-task results that allow readers to audit which task families contributed the gain, and (d) a statistical test that respects the n=21 task structure (paired Wilcoxon plus bootstrap CI). The design below enforces all four.\n\n## 3. Population (BBH Evaluation Items)\n\n- **Unit of analysis:** a single BBH evaluation example.\n- **Tasks:** the **21-task BBH subset defined in Yang et al. 2023 Appendix** (the canonical Suzgun et al. 2022 BBH, restricted to tasks whose target answer is a single string — i.e., excluding `penguins_in_a_table`, `geometric_shapes`, `snarks`, and one additional task whose answer format is incompatible with the OPRO paper's protocol; the exact list is taken verbatim from Yang et al. 2023, Appendix, and not reconstructed here).\n- **Examples per task:** the **250-example per-task evaluation split** released with BIG-Bench Hard. Total N = 21 × 250 = **5,250 evaluation items**.\n- **Source file order:** frozen at the index order shipped in the BBH release; the same indexing is used across all three conditions.\n- **Sample exclusions (pre-registered):** none. Every example is scored under every condition.\n\n## 4. Conditions (3 cells, within-subjects)\n\n| Cell | Instruction prefix | Source |\n|---|---|---|\n| `C0` (empty) | `\"\"` | trivially the empty string |\n| `C1` (CoT trigger) | `\"Let's think step by step.\"` | Kojima et al. 2022 zero-shot CoT trigger |\n| `C2` (OPRO) | per-task optimized instruction string for the given BBH task | Yang et al. 2023, Appendix, Table of optimized instructions |\n\n**Block design.** Each task is run in its own block; within a task, every example is rendered once per condition, in interleaved order to absorb any drift in model state (when an API is used) or in compute warmth (when local weights are used). Each model check is run independently per condition-cell but with the **deterministic, identical prompt string** apart from the instruction prefix.\n\n**Optional 4th cell (documented but not in primary inference):** the universal/cross-task OPRO instruction from Yang et al. 2023, included only as a robustness probe and reported in a separate subsection.\n\n## 5. Controls\n\n1. **Identical prompts modulo prefix.** Every other component of the prompt (few-shot block, question rendering, answer-output cue, trailing whitespace) is byte-identical across `C0`/`C1`/`C2`.\n2. **Identical answer extractor.** The same per-task parser runs on every model's output for every condition.\n3. **Identical eval split.** The same 250 indices per task, in the same order.\n4. **Frozen OPRO instructions.** The published per-task instructions are read from a single CSV `opro_per_task_instructions.csv` (one row per BBH task) and are not edited or re-generated. The CSV is committed to the experiment repository before any model call.\n5. **Greedy decoding.** Removes sampling variance as a confound.\n6. **Symmetric question rendering.** No conditional formatting, no list bullets, no escaping that depends on the prefix.\n7. **Order randomization within a task is disabled**, so condition-effects are not entangled with item order; instead, the same fixed interleaved order is used and reported.\n8. **No LLM-as-judge.** BBH has scalar ground-truth; we use exact match.\n9. **No test-label leakage into prompts.** The OPRO instructions come from a published paper; they were not fit to the BBH eval items in this experiment.\n10. (Optional control contrast in H0) The `\"\"` vs. `\"Let's think step by step.\"` contrast itself — confirms that the well-known CoT advantage manifests in our setup, so we know we are running a non-trivial baseline.\n\n## 6. Materials\n\n### 6.1 BBH evaluation data\n- **Primary release path:** `lukaemon/bbh` on HuggingFace, evaluating the existing per-task 250-example splits.\n- **Cross-check release path:** the original `suzgunmirac/BIG-Bench-Hard` repo files — used to verify item-level alignment with the primary release. If the two releases differ in item order (a known issue), the experiment uses **lukaemon/bbh** and reports a hash check against the original files.\n- **File-level integrity:** SHA-256 hashes of each per-task JSONL file are recorded in the run manifest and committed.\n\n### 6.2 OPRO per-task instructions\n- **Source:** Yang et al. 2023, Appendix, \"Optimized instructions for BBH tasks\". Read into `opro_per_task_instructions.csv` with columns `{task, instruction_text, source_paper_section}`. The CSV is committed before model calls. (The universal instruction, if used in the optional 4th cell, is stored in `opro_universal_instruction.txt`.)\n\n### 6.3 Baselines\n- `C0` empty: `instruction_text = \"\"`.\n- `C1` CoT trigger: `instruction_text = \"Let's think step by step.\"` (no trailing space; the prompt builder handles concatenation).\n- `C2` OPRO: `instruction_text = row[task].instruction_text`.\n\n### 6.4 Model\n- **Primary:** instruction-tuned **PaLM 2-L**, accessed via the model ID used in the OPRO paper. (We treat this as a fixed constant; researchers running on a closed model can substitute `text-bison-001` or `gpt-3.5-turbo` as a parallel arm if reproducibility allows.)\n- **Model identifier** is recorded in the run manifest, alongside `model_revision` / API version / commit hash.\n\n### 6.5 Compute target\n- **Default compute target:** hosted Google / OpenAI model API with greedy decoding and a deterministic seed.\n- **Alternative compute target:** locally hosted open-weights instruction-tuned model (e.g., Llama-3.x-Instruct) on GPU. The choice does **not** change the protocol; only the manifest entries change.\n\n### 6.6 Software stack (runnable in an external environment)\n- Python ≥ 3.10; `huggingface_datasets` ≥ 2.14; `pandas`; `scipy.stats.wilcoxon`; `numpy`/`bootstrapped` for paired bootstrap; `tqdm`.\n- `git`-tracked experiment repo: `src/build_prompts.py`, `src/run_inference.py`, `src/score.py`, `src/analyze.py`, `data/opro_per_task_instructions.csv`, `results/<run_id>/{predictions,scores,analysis}.jsonl|md`.\n\n## 7. Prompt-Construction Protocol\n\nFor each `(task, example, condition)`:\n\n```\nprompt = instruction_prefix + \"\\n\\n\" + few_shot_block + question + answer_output_cue\n```\n\nWhere:\n\n- `instruction_prefix` is the condition-specific string (empty / CoT trigger / OPRO).\n- `few_shot_block` is empty (`\"\"`) — zero-shot condition, held constant across cells.\n- `question` is the BBH example's \"Question: …\" / \"Q: …\" text, rendered byte-identically across cells.\n- `answer_output_cue` is the per-task answer-format directive **bound to the instruction prefix** so that the empty cell has no cue (this matches the OPRO framework; the cue travels with the instruction):\n  - yes/no tasks → `\"Answer with one of: yes, no.\"`\n  - multi-choice (A/B/C/…) → `\"Answer with one of: A, B, C, D, ...\"` (one item per choice option).\n  - free-form string → no cue.\n  - For the empty cell (`\"\"`), `answer_output_cue` is empty too, by the binding convention.\n\nThis convention is itself a design choice and is fixed before unblinding; it ensures that the empty cell is genuinely instruction-less.\n\nThe decoder's continuation (`model output`) is captured verbatim to disk.\n\n## 8. Decoding and Compute Configuration\n\n- **Sampling:** greedy. `temperature = 0.0`, `top_p = 1.0`, `top_k` not constrained (or `-1`/∞), `repetition_penalty = 1.0`.\n- **Max new tokens:** 512 for short-form tasks, 1024 for `dyck_languages` / `word_sorting`. Set per task.\n- **Stop sequences:** per-task — task end-of-string marker where defined.\n- **Seed:** fixed (`42`) for any model that accepts a seed; recorded.\n- **Seeds N:** N = 1 by default (greedy is deterministic). If the model cannot guarantee greedy determinism (some APIs), the run is repeated with N = 3 seeds (`{42, 43, 44}`); the recorded score per cell is the macro-mean across seeds, and the **median-seed** scores are used for the paired Wilcoxon test.\n- **Batching:** fully batched per task × condition; no per-example bells-and-whistles that would diverge from greedy behavior.\n- **Rate limits / retry:** capped exponential backoff; no more than 5 retries per item; logged.\n- **Cost estimate:** 21 tasks × 250 items × 3 conditions = 15,750 generations. At ~250 output tokens average, ≈ 3.9M output tokens per model, ≈ 7.8M total tokens (incl. prompt). Within reach of any credit-tier PaLM 2-L / GPT-class API budget, but infeasible in this bubblewrap.\n\n## 9. Metrics\n\n**Primary metric — exact-match (EM) accuracy.**\n\nFor each `(task, example, condition)`:\n\n```\npred_text = last 256 tokens of the model's completion, stripped of leading \"Answer:\" / \"Final answer:\" tokens and trailing punctuation\ngold = row.target.strip()\ny_hat = normalize(pred_text, task)       # task-specific normalizer\ny     = normalize(gold, task)\ncorrect = (y_hat == y)\n```\n\n**Per-task normalizers (pre-registered):**\n- yes/no tasks → lowercase, strip whitespace, collapse to `{yes, no}`.\n- multiple-choice (A/B/C/…) → extract first uppercase letter that appears as a standalone token; if none, extract the option text in lowercase and look up its letter.\n- numeric tasks (object_counting, multistep_arithmetic_two) → extract the last integer in the response; no float tolerance.\n- date tasks (date_understanding) → match DD/MM/YYYY or YYYY-MM-DD after reformatting to a canonical string.\n- list-of-words tasks that survived the 21-task filter (e.g., word_sorting) → split on whitespace, sort, lowercase, compare the joined result. (Tasks whose answer is a set/list of strings are excluded by the 21-task definition.)\n- all other free-form tasks → case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed, punctuation-stripped substring match against the gold string.\n\n**Per-cell score (per task):**\n`acc(task, condition) = (1/250) × Σ correct`\n\n**Macro score (per condition):**\n`μ(condition) = (1/21) × Σ acc(task, condition)`\n\n**Secondary metrics (reported alongside, not used for inference):**\n- Median per-task accuracy.\n- Per-task macro-delta `Δ_task = acc(task, OPRO) − acc(task, baseline)`.\n- Per-task correctly-predicted-counts heatmap.\n\n## 10. Statistical Analysis Plan\n\n### 10.1 Primary inference\n\nFor each of the two pre-registered pairwise contrasts, build a vector of per-task accuracy differences `d_t = acc(t, OPRO) − acc(t, baseline)` over the 21 tasks.\n\n- **Test statistic:** one-sided paired Wilcoxon signed-rank on `d_t` (positive alternative for \"OPRO > baseline\").\n- **Significance threshold:** α = 0.025 per contrast (Bonferroni splits 0.05 across H1 and H2).\n- **Effect size:** matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation `r_rb = (W⁺ − W⁻) / (W⁺ + W⁻)` where `W⁺, W⁻` are Wilcoxon positive/negative rank sums. Cliff's δ also reported.\n- **Macro-delta:** `Δ = μ(OPRO) − μ(baseline)` with a paired bootstrap 95% CI: resample the 21 task-level `(acc_OPRO, acc_baseline)` pairs 10,000 times with replacement; record the 2.5th/97.5th percentile of the resampled `Δ`. **Decision rule:** support the hypothesis if the lower 95% CI bound of `Δ` is positive.\n\n### 10.2 Secondary inference (exploratory, multiple-comparison-protected only within family)\n\n- Per-task two-sided McNemar test on per-example correctness flips (OPRO vs. each baseline), pooled at α = 0.05 with Benjamini-Hochberg FDR over the 21 task family.\n- Per-task-family aggregated score (numeric, multi-choice, yes/no, symbolic) — reported as a stratified table.\n\n### 10.3 Robustness checks (pre-registered)\n\n- **Test alternative baseline:** repeat H1 and H2 using the per-task OPRO instruction's length-matched random-instruction baseline (a non-OPRO instruction of the same token length, drawn from a fixed candidate list). This addresses \"OPRO only wins because optimized instructions are longer / better-formed.\"\n- **Test few-shot variant:** repeat H1/H2 with a 3-shot CoT prefix added identically to all three cells (as a secondary analysis cell). Confirms instruction effect is not an artifact of zero-shot.\n\n### 10.4 Output artifacts\n\n- `scores.jsonl`: one row per `(task, condition)` with `acc`, `n`, `delta_vs_OPRO`.\n- `analysis.md`: macro table, per-task table, Wilcoxon p-values (raw + Bonferroni), effect sizes, bootstrap CIs.\n- `figures/`: per-task accuracy bars, macro-delta forest plot, Δ_bbh heatmap.\n\n## 11. Pre-Registration\n\nPre-registered (i.e., fixed before any model call or scoring) elements:\n\n1. Hypotheses H1, H2, and the exploratory H0 (their one-sidedness, contrast direction, and α).\n2. The 21-task subset and the per-task normalizer table.\n3. The empty-prefix convention for `\"\"` (no answer-output cue).\n4. The decoding settings (greedy, max tokens).\n5. The number of items per task (250).\n6. The significance threshold family and correction (Bonferroni α = 0.025 per primary contrast).\n7. The bootstrap resample count (10,000) and seed (42).\n8. The robustness re-runs (length-matched baseline, few-shot variant).\n\nPre-registration artifact: a `PREREGISTRATION.md` in the experiment repo, with timestamp, git commit SHA, and a hash of the in-scope task list and OPRO instruction CSV. Any deviation after unblinding is recorded in `DEVIATIONS.md` with rationale.\n\n## 12. Feasibility\n\nThis design **cannot be executed** in the current bubblewrap environment:\n- No GPU.\n- Network disabled.\n- No model API credentials or weights.\n- No BBH dataset pre-staged.\n\nIn an external compute/API environment, feasibility is high:\n\n- **Number of generative calls:** 15,750 (3 conditions × 21 tasks × 250 items), plus the optional 4th cell and robustness re-runs ⇒ ≤ ~80k calls.\n- **Token budget:** ≈ 8M output tokens + ≈ 2× input tokens ⇒ ≤ ~24M total tokens for the primary analysis.\n- **Wall-clock time:** ≈ 1.5–6 hours on a single hosted API at modest QPS; ~30–60 minutes on a local 70B-class GPU server in bf16/fp16.\n- **Cost:** within the order of a single-digit USD amount per hosted model for the primary analysis, ~10× for full robustness.\n- **Storage:** ≤ 200 MB of model completions and score JSONLs.\n\nResource pre-requisites: API key + a hosted PaLM 2-L endpoint (or equivalent), BBH data downloaded once (~5 MB), the pre-registered CSV of OPRO instructions downloaded once (~50 KB).\n\n## 13. Limitations\n\n1. **Single-model evidence.** Primary panel is PaLM 2-L; conclusions may not transfer unchanged to a different instruction-tuned family. The optional cross-family arm is reported but is not the primary inference.\n2. **Reuse of published OPRO instructions, not re-derivation.** The paper's own optimal instructions are taken at face value; any bugs in those instructions transfer to us. We do not re-optimize, by feasibility.\n3. **Order effects in OPRO optimization.** Yang et al. 2023 note that OPRO's output depends on the seed of the optimizer LLM (not the inference LLM); we therefore inherit a dependence on whatever the published per-task instructions chose. This is acknowledged but not corrected for.\n4. **Possible train/eval contamination.** BBH examples were public; if the model under test was trained on them (likely for some families), EM is an upper-bound estimate of the true instruction effect, not a clean measure. We mitigate only via the published OPRO paper's protocol, which used the same model.\n5. **n_tasks = 21 is modest.** Wilcoxon has low power at this n; a null result cannot exclude small but real instruction effects. Pre-registration sets this explicitly.\n6. **Macro-mean weighting.** Tasks are not weighted by difficulty or length; results are unweighted means. A difficulty-weighted sensitivity check is included in the secondary output.\n7. **Empty-baseline bias choice.** Treating `\"\"` as having no answer-output cue is a defensible convention (it asks the model nothing), but a stricter baseline would include the cue without the CoT trigger. We note both and report a small sensitivity analysis in `analysis.md`.\n8. **No LLM-as-judge.** Tasks whose gold answer is fuzzy (none in the 21-task subset) would require one; BBH does not, so this is not a current limitation.\n9. **Network/environment.** This bubblewrap is sandboxed; execution must occur elsewhere, which makes strict on-platform reproducibility impossible here.\n\n## 14. Deliverables (when the experiment is run later, in an external environment)\n\n1. `PREREGISTRATION.md`, `opro_per_task_instructions.csv` (with SHA-256).\n2. `prompts/<task>.jsonl` per task — fully rendered prompts (1 per example per condition) for audit.\n3. `raw/<task>__<condition>.jsonl` — model completions.\n4. `scores.jsonl` — per-task per-condition EM accuracy.\n5. `analysis.md` — macro table, per-task table, Wilcoxon tests, Bonferroni-corrected p's, macro-delta bootstrap 95% CIs, effect sizes, robustness re-runs.\n6. `figures/` — per-task accuracy bars, Δ heatmap, macro-delta forest plot.\n7. `DEVIATIONS.md` + run manifest with model ID, decode params, seeds.\n\n## 15. Concrete One-Page Run-Plan Reference (for the executing environment)\n\n```\n1. git init experiment/\n2. Place BBH JSONL files in data/bbh/<task>.jsonl; sha256sum > data/bbh.sha256\n3. Place opro_per_task_instructions.csv in data/; sha256sum > data/opro.sha256\n4. Export PREREGISTRATION.md\n5. python src/build_prompts.py --tasks src/task_list.txt --conditions C0,C1,C2 \\\n       --opro_csv data/opro_per_task_instructions.csv \\\n       --out prompts/ --seed 42\n6. python src/run_inference.py --prompts prompts/ \\\n       --model <MODEL_ID> --temperature 0 --max_tokens_per_task src/max_tokens.csv \\\n       --seeds 42,43,44 --out raw/\n7. python src/score.py --predictions raw/ --gold data/bbh/ --tasks src/task_list.txt \\\n       --out scores.jsonl\n8. python src/analyze.py --scores scores.jsonl --out analysis.md --boot_n 10000 --seed 42\n9. git add . && git commit -m \"run: <sha run_id>\"\n```\n\n---\n\n**Closing note:** The repository supplied to this design contains only `task.json` and the literal pointer string `\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\"` Every artifact in this design (BBH data, OPRO instructions, model checkpoints/APIs) must be obtained from public sources or external compute. No element of the design depends on hidden in-repo materials, and no part of this document claims that any experimental run has occurred in the current sandbox.", "evidence": "(1) Repository inspection is unchanged: /workspace contains exactly task.json (7 lines, 445 bytes); its documents.track.md is the literal string \"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\" No source code, configs, scripts, READMEs, manifests, OPRO artifacts, or BBH artifacts are present. /home and ~ are empty; no project tree exists under /. find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f returned only that one file. (2) Environment inspection: nvidia-smi reports no NVIDIA driver (no GPU); curl https://www.google.com with -m 2 returned HTTP 000 (network disabled); system Python on Linux, 24 CPUs, no project dependencies installed; task.json states datasets: []. (3) Therefore all data, instructions, and model references in this design (BBH data from Suzgun et al. 2022 / lukaemon/bbh; OPRO per-task instructions from Yang et al. 2023 Appendix; PaLM 2-L model endpoint) trace to publicly available materials or to an external execution environment. (4) No experimental run has been performed in this sandbox; the deliverable above is a self-contained design-only document grounded in the locked defaults from the approved plan (per-task OPRO instructions; 21-task OPRO subset; PaLM 2-L; zero-shot; greedy decoding; answer-style cue bound to the instruction prefix; Bonferroni α=0.025/contrast; paired Wilcoxon plus paired bootstrap CI). (5) Every item that would require a model call or external data is listed explicitly as such and tagged with the runnable step that would invoke it in an external environment.", "assumptions": ["Reference OPRO paper is Yang et al. 2023 Large Language Models as Optimizers; per-task optimized instructions are sourced verbatim from its Appendix and frozen as a CSV before any model call.", "Reference BBH is Suzgun et al. 2022 BIG-Bench Hard; the 21-task subset is defined by Yang et al. 2023 Appendix (single-string-answer subset of Suzgun's 23) and is read as the exact task list published with the paper rather than reconstructed.", "Eval split is the 250-example per-task BBH evaluation split, downloaded from lukaemon/bbh (HuggingFace) with hash-check against the original Suzgun release.", "Primary model is instruction-tuned PaLM 2-L via the OPRO paper's API; substitute models (text-bison-001, gpt-3.5-turbo, open-weights instruction-tuned models) are documented as parallel arms.", "Decoding is greedy at temperature 0.0 with top_p 1.0; the recorded score per cell is the macro-mean of 3 seeds if non-determinism is detected, otherwise the single deterministic pass.", "Few-shot = zero-shot; held constant across all three conditions.", "The answer-output cue (e.g., \"Answer with one of: yes, no.\") is bound to the instruction prefix so the empty cell carries no cue; this convention is fixed before unblinding.", "Scoring is exact-match after per-task normalization, defined ahead of time in this document; no LLM-as-judge is introduced.", "Inference is paired Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-task accuracy deltas with Bonferroni-corrected alpha = 0.025 per primary contrast, supplemented by a paired bootstrap 95% CI on macro-deltas; robustness re-runs include a length-matched random-instruction baseline and a 3-shot few-shot variant.", "Execution is infeasible in this bubblewrap (no GPU, network off, no model/API, no data) and is therefore deferred to an external compute environment; this design documents only the protocol and analysis plan."], "uncertainties": ["Exact identity of the 21st-vs-23rd-and-27th BIG-Bench Hard task lists: this design defers to Yang et al. 2023 Appendix for the canonical 21-task list rather than re-enumerating here.", "Whether the hosted PaLM 2-L endpoint accepts a true greedy mode; if it does not, the design falls back to N=3 seeds and uses the median seed for the Wilcoxon test.", "Whether the BBH lukaemon/bbh release matches the original Suzgun files item-for-item; SHA-256 cross-check is in the run manifest but the experiment proceeds if the hashes agree on the per-task item text.", "Whether the published per-task OPRO instructions in Yang et al. 2023 contain any cases of implicit test-set overfitting; addressed only via reporting per-task ∆ and the falsification criterion in Section 1.", "Whether to add the universal OPRO instruction as a primary or robustness cell; default is robust-only (Section 4), not part of primary inference.", "Choice of post-correction method of multiple-testing across the 21 task-family McNemar tests; default is Benjamini-Hochberg FDR within the 21-task family (secondary inference only)."], "kind": "ReviewHandoffAction"}, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "call-ca62c0e8-42b8-48eb-9c35-8d63d3e443bf", "tool_call": {"id": "call-ca62c0e8-42b8-48eb-9c35-8d63d3e443bf", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{\"summary\": \"Final design document (synthesis) covering all required sections\", \"stage\": \"result\", \"answer\": \"# Final Design Document \\u2014 OPRO vs. Baselines on BBH\\n\\n> **Status:** Design-only. No experiment has been executed in this bubblewrap (no GPU, network disabled, no model/API). The design below is self-contained and runnable in an environment that has access to the chosen model and the BBH data.\\n\\n---\\n\\n## 0. Executive Summary\\n\\nWe will run a within-subjects, three-condition head-to-head evaluation on the 21-task OPRO subset of BIG-Bench Hard (BBH). Each BBH evaluation example is scored under (i) the empty instruction \\\"\\\", (ii) \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\", and (iii) the per-task OPRO-optimized instruction published in Yang et al. 2023 Appendix. The model under test is instruction-tuned PaLM 2-L; decoding is greedy (T=0.0); scoring is exact-match with per-task normalization. Primary inference is paired Wilcoxon signed-rank over 21 task accuracies with Bonferroni-corrected \\u03b1 = 0.05/3, supplemented by a paired bootstrap 95% CI on macro-deltas. Pre-registered hypotheses: H1 \\u2014 OPRO > \\\"\\\", H2 \\u2014 OPRO > \\\"step by step.\\\", each on macro-accuracy.\\n\\n## 1. Scientific Question and Pre-Registered Hypotheses\\n\\n**Research question:** Do the optimized instructions discovered by OPRO outperform the baseline instructions (`\\\"\\\"` and `\\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"`) on BBH tasks?\\n\\n**Pre-registered hypotheses** (binary, pre-specified before data inspection):\\n\\n- **H1:** \\u03bc_OPRO > \\u03bc_empty on macro BBH accuracy (21-task mean).\\n- **H2:** \\u03bc_OPRO > \\u03bc_step-by-step on macro BBH accuracy.\\n- **H0 (control contrast, exploratory):** \\u03bc_empty = \\u03bc_step-by-step \\u2014 included to verify the well-known finding that \\\"step by step\\\" beats the empty prompt under zero-shot CoT; if H0 is rejected with empty < step-by-step, the baseline ordering of H1/H2 is sensible.\\n\\n**Decision rule:** A hypothesis is declared supported if its one-sided Wilcoxon p-value (corrected for 2 primary contrasts) is below \\u03b1 = 0.025 (Bonferroni split of 0.05 across H1 and H2) **and** the bootstrap CI on the macro-delta excludes zero.\\n\\n**Falsification criteria (would refute the headline claim):**\\n- H1 not supported (\\u0394 macro \\u2264 0, p \\u2265 \\u03b1), or\\n- H2 not supported (\\u0394 macro \\u2264 0, p \\u2265 \\u03b1), or\\n- Per-task inspection shows OPRO wins only on tasks where the per-task instruction was clearly overfit (e.g., contains task-specific keywords), suggesting leakage.\\n\\n## 2. Rationale\\n\\nThe OPRO paper's central empirical claim is that LLM-optimized instruction prefixes exceed hand-crafted zero-shot CoT triggers on BBH. That claim has been reported in one paper with specific models. A rigorous confirmation requires: (a) identical prompts modulo the instruction prefix, (b) a paired per-item evaluation so task-level differences cancel, (c) per-task results that allow readers to audit which task families contributed the gain, and (d) a statistical test that respects the n=21 task structure (paired Wilcoxon plus bootstrap CI). The design below enforces all four.\\n\\n## 3. Population (BBH Evaluation Items)\\n\\n- **Unit of analysis:** a single BBH evaluation example.\\n- **Tasks:** the **21-task BBH subset defined in Yang et al. 2023 Appendix** (the canonical Suzgun et al. 2022 BBH, restricted to tasks whose target answer is a single string \\u2014 i.e., excluding `penguins_in_a_table`, `geometric_shapes`, `snarks`, and one additional task whose answer format is incompatible with the OPRO paper's protocol; the exact list is taken verbatim from Yang et al. 2023, Appendix, and not reconstructed here).\\n- **Examples per task:** the **250-example per-task evaluation split** released with BIG-Bench Hard. Total N = 21 \\u00d7 250 = **5,250 evaluation items**.\\n- **Source file order:** frozen at the index order shipped in the BBH release; the same indexing is used across all three conditions.\\n- **Sample exclusions (pre-registered):** none. Every example is scored under every condition.\\n\\n## 4. Conditions (3 cells, within-subjects)\\n\\n| Cell | Instruction prefix | Source |\\n|---|---|---|\\n| `C0` (empty) | `\\\"\\\"` | trivially the empty string |\\n| `C1` (CoT trigger) | `\\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"` | Kojima et al. 2022 zero-shot CoT trigger |\\n| `C2` (OPRO) | per-task optimized instruction string for the given BBH task | Yang et al. 2023, Appendix, Table of optimized instructions |\\n\\n**Block design.** Each task is run in its own block; within a task, every example is rendered once per condition, in interleaved order to absorb any drift in model state (when an API is used) or in compute warmth (when local weights are used). Each model check is run independently per condition-cell but with the **deterministic, identical prompt string** apart from the instruction prefix.\\n\\n**Optional 4th cell (documented but not in primary inference):** the universal/cross-task OPRO instruction from Yang et al. 2023, included only as a robustness probe and reported in a separate subsection.\\n\\n## 5. Controls\\n\\n1. **Identical prompts modulo prefix.** Every other component of the prompt (few-shot block, question rendering, answer-output cue, trailing whitespace) is byte-identical across `C0`/`C1`/`C2`.\\n2. **Identical answer extractor.** The same per-task parser runs on every model's output for every condition.\\n3. **Identical eval split.** The same 250 indices per task, in the same order.\\n4. **Frozen OPRO instructions.** The published per-task instructions are read from a single CSV `opro_per_task_instructions.csv` (one row per BBH task) and are not edited or re-generated. The CSV is committed to the experiment repository before any model call.\\n5. **Greedy decoding.** Removes sampling variance as a confound.\\n6. **Symmetric question rendering.** No conditional formatting, no list bullets, no escaping that depends on the prefix.\\n7. **Order randomization within a task is disabled**, so condition-effects are not entangled with item order; instead, the same fixed interleaved order is used and reported.\\n8. **No LLM-as-judge.** BBH has scalar ground-truth; we use exact match.\\n9. **No test-label leakage into prompts.** The OPRO instructions come from a published paper; they were not fit to the BBH eval items in this experiment.\\n10. (Optional control contrast in H0) The `\\\"\\\"` vs. `\\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"` contrast itself \\u2014 confirms that the well-known CoT advantage manifests in our setup, so we know we are running a non-trivial baseline.\\n\\n## 6. Materials\\n\\n### 6.1 BBH evaluation data\\n- **Primary release path:** `lukaemon/bbh` on HuggingFace, evaluating the existing per-task 250-example splits.\\n- **Cross-check release path:** the original `suzgunmirac/BIG-Bench-Hard` repo files \\u2014 used to verify item-level alignment with the primary release. If the two releases differ in item order (a known issue), the experiment uses **lukaemon/bbh** and reports a hash check against the original files.\\n- **File-level integrity:** SHA-256 hashes of each per-task JSONL file are recorded in the run manifest and committed.\\n\\n### 6.2 OPRO per-task instructions\\n- **Source:** Yang et al. 2023, Appendix, \\\"Optimized instructions for BBH tasks\\\". Read into `opro_per_task_instructions.csv` with columns `{task, instruction_text, source_paper_section}`. The CSV is committed before model calls. (The universal instruction, if used in the optional 4th cell, is stored in `opro_universal_instruction.txt`.)\\n\\n### 6.3 Baselines\\n- `C0` empty: `instruction_text = \\\"\\\"`.\\n- `C1` CoT trigger: `instruction_text = \\\"Let's think step by step.\\\"` (no trailing space; the prompt builder handles concatenation).\\n- `C2` OPRO: `instruction_text = row[task].instruction_text`.\\n\\n### 6.4 Model\\n- **Primary:** instruction-tuned **PaLM 2-L**, accessed via the model ID used in the OPRO paper. (We treat this as a fixed constant; researchers running on a closed model can substitute `text-bison-001` or `gpt-3.5-turbo` as a parallel arm if reproducibility allows.)\\n- **Model identifier** is recorded in the run manifest, alongside `model_revision` / API version / commit hash.\\n\\n### 6.5 Compute target\\n- **Default compute target:** hosted Google / OpenAI model API with greedy decoding and a deterministic seed.\\n- **Alternative compute target:** locally hosted open-weights instruction-tuned model (e.g., Llama-3.x-Instruct) on GPU. The choice does **not** change the protocol; only the manifest entries change.\\n\\n### 6.6 Software stack (runnable in an external environment)\\n- Python \\u2265 3.10; `huggingface_datasets` \\u2265 2.14; `pandas`; `scipy.stats.wilcoxon`; `numpy`/`bootstrapped` for paired bootstrap; `tqdm`.\\n- `git`-tracked experiment repo: `src/build_prompts.py`, `src/run_inference.py`, `src/score.py`, `src/analyze.py`, `data/opro_per_task_instructions.csv`, `results/<run_id>/{predictions,scores,analysis}.jsonl|md`.\\n\\n## 7. Prompt-Construction Protocol\\n\\nFor each `(task, example, condition)`:\\n\\n```\\nprompt = instruction_prefix + \\\"\\\\n\\\\n\\\" + few_shot_block + question + answer_output_cue\\n```\\n\\nWhere:\\n\\n- `instruction_prefix` is the condition-specific string (empty / CoT trigger / OPRO).\\n- `few_shot_block` is empty (`\\\"\\\"`) \\u2014 zero-shot condition, held constant across cells.\\n- `question` is the BBH example's \\\"Question: \\u2026\\\" / \\\"Q: \\u2026\\\" text, rendered byte-identically across cells.\\n- `answer_output_cue` is the per-task answer-format directive **bound to the instruction prefix** so that the empty cell has no cue (this matches the OPRO framework; the cue travels with the instruction):\\n  - yes/no tasks \\u2192 `\\\"Answer with one of: yes, no.\\\"`\\n  - multi-choice (A/B/C/\\u2026) \\u2192 `\\\"Answer with one of: A, B, C, D, ...\\\"` (one item per choice option).\\n  - free-form string \\u2192 no cue.\\n  - For the empty cell (`\\\"\\\"`), `answer_output_cue` is empty too, by the binding convention.\\n\\nThis convention is itself a design choice and is fixed before unblinding; it ensures that the empty cell is genuinely instruction-less.\\n\\nThe decoder's continuation (`model output`) is captured verbatim to disk.\\n\\n## 8. Decoding and Compute Configuration\\n\\n- **Sampling:** greedy. `temperature = 0.0`, `top_p = 1.0`, `top_k` not constrained (or `-1`/\\u221e), `repetition_penalty = 1.0`.\\n- **Max new tokens:** 512 for short-form tasks, 1024 for `dyck_languages` / `word_sorting`. Set per task.\\n- **Stop sequences:** per-task \\u2014 task end-of-string marker where defined.\\n- **Seed:** fixed (`42`) for any model that accepts a seed; recorded.\\n- **Seeds N:** N = 1 by default (greedy is deterministic). If the model cannot guarantee greedy determinism (some APIs), the run is repeated with N = 3 seeds (`{42, 43, 44}`); the recorded score per cell is the macro-mean across seeds, and the **median-seed** scores are used for the paired Wilcoxon test.\\n- **Batching:** fully batched per task \\u00d7 condition; no per-example bells-and-whistles that would diverge from greedy behavior.\\n- **Rate limits / retry:** capped exponential backoff; no more than 5 retries per item; logged.\\n- **Cost estimate:** 21 tasks \\u00d7 250 items \\u00d7 3 conditions = 15,750 generations. At ~250 output tokens average, \\u2248 3.9M output tokens per model, \\u2248 7.8M total tokens (incl. prompt). Within reach of any credit-tier PaLM 2-L / GPT-class API budget, but infeasible in this bubblewrap.\\n\\n## 9. Metrics\\n\\n**Primary metric \\u2014 exact-match (EM) accuracy.**\\n\\nFor each `(task, example, condition)`:\\n\\n```\\npred_text = last 256 tokens of the model's completion, stripped of leading \\\"Answer:\\\" / \\\"Final answer:\\\" tokens and trailing punctuation\\ngold = row.target.strip()\\ny_hat = normalize(pred_text, task)       # task-specific normalizer\\ny     = normalize(gold, task)\\ncorrect = (y_hat == y)\\n```\\n\\n**Per-task normalizers (pre-registered):**\\n- yes/no tasks \\u2192 lowercase, strip whitespace, collapse to `{yes, no}`.\\n- multiple-choice (A/B/C/\\u2026) \\u2192 extract first uppercase letter that appears as a standalone token; if none, extract the option text in lowercase and look up its letter.\\n- numeric tasks (object_counting, multistep_arithmetic_two) \\u2192 extract the last integer in the response; no float tolerance.\\n- date tasks (date_understanding) \\u2192 match DD/MM/YYYY or YYYY-MM-DD after reformatting to a canonical string.\\n- list-of-words tasks that survived the 21-task filter (e.g., word_sorting) \\u2192 split on whitespace, sort, lowercase, compare the joined result. (Tasks whose answer is a set/list of strings are excluded by the 21-task definition.)\\n- all other free-form tasks \\u2192 case-insensitive, whitespace-collapsed, punctuation-stripped substring match against the gold string.\\n\\n**Per-cell score (per task):**\\n`acc(task, condition) = (1/250) \\u00d7 \\u03a3 correct`\\n\\n**Macro score (per condition):**\\n`\\u03bc(condition) = (1/21) \\u00d7 \\u03a3 acc(task, condition)`\\n\\n**Secondary metrics (reported alongside, not used for inference):**\\n- Median per-task accuracy.\\n- Per-task macro-delta `\\u0394_task = acc(task, OPRO) \\u2212 acc(task, baseline)`.\\n- Per-task correctly-predicted-counts heatmap.\\n\\n## 10. Statistical Analysis Plan\\n\\n### 10.1 Primary inference\\n\\nFor each of the two pre-registered pairwise contrasts, build a vector of per-task accuracy differences `d_t = acc(t, OPRO) \\u2212 acc(t, baseline)` over the 21 tasks.\\n\\n- **Test statistic:** one-sided paired Wilcoxon signed-rank on `d_t` (positive alternative for \\\"OPRO > baseline\\\").\\n- **Significance threshold:** \\u03b1 = 0.025 per contrast (Bonferroni splits 0.05 across H1 and H2).\\n- **Effect size:** matched-pairs rank-biserial correlation `r_rb = (W\\u207a \\u2212 W\\u207b) / (W\\u207a + W\\u207b)` where `W\\u207a, W\\u207b` are Wilcoxon positive/negative rank sums. Cliff's \\u03b4 also reported.\\n- **Macro-delta:** `\\u0394 = \\u03bc(OPRO) \\u2212 \\u03bc(baseline)` with a paired bootstrap 95% CI: resample the 21 task-level `(acc_OPRO, acc_baseline)` pairs 10,000 times with replacement; record the 2.5th/97.5th percentile of the resampled `\\u0394`. **Decision rule:** support the hypothesis if the lower 95% CI bound of `\\u0394` is positive.\\n\\n### 10.2 Secondary inference (exploratory, multiple-comparison-protected only within family)\\n\\n- Per-task two-sided McNemar test on per-example correctness flips (OPRO vs. each baseline), pooled at \\u03b1 = 0.05 with Benjamini-Hochberg FDR over the 21 task family.\\n- Per-task-family aggregated score (numeric, multi-choice, yes/no, symbolic) \\u2014 reported as a stratified table.\\n\\n### 10.3 Robustness checks (pre-registered)\\n\\n- **Test alternative baseline:** repeat H1 and H2 using the per-task OPRO instruction's length-matched random-instruction baseline (a non-OPRO instruction of the same token length, drawn from a fixed candidate list). This addresses \\\"OPRO only wins because optimized instructions are longer / better-formed.\\\"\\n- **Test few-shot variant:** repeat H1/H2 with a 3-shot CoT prefix added identically to all three cells (as a secondary analysis cell). Confirms instruction effect is not an artifact of zero-shot.\\n\\n### 10.4 Output artifacts\\n\\n- `scores.jsonl`: one row per `(task, condition)` with `acc`, `n`, `delta_vs_OPRO`.\\n- `analysis.md`: macro table, per-task table, Wilcoxon p-values (raw + Bonferroni), effect sizes, bootstrap CIs.\\n- `figures/`: per-task accuracy bars, macro-delta forest plot, \\u0394_bbh heatmap.\\n\\n## 11. Pre-Registration\\n\\nPre-registered (i.e., fixed before any model call or scoring) elements:\\n\\n1. Hypotheses H1, H2, and the exploratory H0 (their one-sidedness, contrast direction, and \\u03b1).\\n2. The 21-task subset and the per-task normalizer table.\\n3. The empty-prefix convention for `\\\"\\\"` (no answer-output cue).\\n4. The decoding settings (greedy, max tokens).\\n5. The number of items per task (250).\\n6. The significance threshold family and correction (Bonferroni \\u03b1 = 0.025 per primary contrast).\\n7. The bootstrap resample count (10,000) and seed (42).\\n8. The robustness re-runs (length-matched baseline, few-shot variant).\\n\\nPre-registration artifact: a `PREREGISTRATION.md` in the experiment repo, with timestamp, git commit SHA, and a hash of the in-scope task list and OPRO instruction CSV. Any deviation after unblinding is recorded in `DEVIATIONS.md` with rationale.\\n\\n## 12. Feasibility\\n\\nThis design **cannot be executed** in the current bubblewrap environment:\\n- No GPU.\\n- Network disabled.\\n- No model API credentials or weights.\\n- No BBH dataset pre-staged.\\n\\nIn an external compute/API environment, feasibility is high:\\n\\n- **Number of generative calls:** 15,750 (3 conditions \\u00d7 21 tasks \\u00d7 250 items), plus the optional 4th cell and robustness re-runs \\u21d2 \\u2264 ~80k calls.\\n- **Token budget:** \\u2248 8M output tokens + \\u2248 2\\u00d7 input tokens \\u21d2 \\u2264 ~24M total tokens for the primary analysis.\\n- **Wall-clock time:** \\u2248 1.5\\u20136 hours on a single hosted API at modest QPS; ~30\\u201360 minutes on a local 70B-class GPU server in bf16/fp16.\\n- **Cost:** within the order of a single-digit USD amount per hosted model for the primary analysis, ~10\\u00d7 for full robustness.\\n- **Storage:** \\u2264 200 MB of model completions and score JSONLs.\\n\\nResource pre-requisites: API key + a hosted PaLM 2-L endpoint (or equivalent), BBH data downloaded once (~5 MB), the pre-registered CSV of OPRO instructions downloaded once (~50 KB).\\n\\n## 13. Limitations\\n\\n1. **Single-model evidence.** Primary panel is PaLM 2-L; conclusions may not transfer unchanged to a different instruction-tuned family. The optional cross-family arm is reported but is not the primary inference.\\n2. **Reuse of published OPRO instructions, not re-derivation.** The paper's own optimal instructions are taken at face value; any bugs in those instructions transfer to us. We do not re-optimize, by feasibility.\\n3. **Order effects in OPRO optimization.** Yang et al. 2023 note that OPRO's output depends on the seed of the optimizer LLM (not the inference LLM); we therefore inherit a dependence on whatever the published per-task instructions chose. This is acknowledged but not corrected for.\\n4. **Possible train/eval contamination.** BBH examples were public; if the model under test was trained on them (likely for some families), EM is an upper-bound estimate of the true instruction effect, not a clean measure. We mitigate only via the published OPRO paper's protocol, which used the same model.\\n5. **n_tasks = 21 is modest.** Wilcoxon has low power at this n; a null result cannot exclude small but real instruction effects. Pre-registration sets this explicitly.\\n6. **Macro-mean weighting.** Tasks are not weighted by difficulty or length; results are unweighted means. A difficulty-weighted sensitivity check is included in the secondary output.\\n7. **Empty-baseline bias choice.** Treating `\\\"\\\"` as having no answer-output cue is a defensible convention (it asks the model nothing), but a stricter baseline would include the cue without the CoT trigger. We note both and report a small sensitivity analysis in `analysis.md`.\\n8. **No LLM-as-judge.** Tasks whose gold answer is fuzzy (none in the 21-task subset) would require one; BBH does not, so this is not a current limitation.\\n9. **Network/environment.** This bubblewrap is sandboxed; execution must occur elsewhere, which makes strict on-platform reproducibility impossible here.\\n\\n## 14. Deliverables (when the experiment is run later, in an external environment)\\n\\n1. `PREREGISTRATION.md`, `opro_per_task_instructions.csv` (with SHA-256).\\n2. `prompts/<task>.jsonl` per task \\u2014 fully rendered prompts (1 per example per condition) for audit.\\n3. `raw/<task>__<condition>.jsonl` \\u2014 model completions.\\n4. `scores.jsonl` \\u2014 per-task per-condition EM accuracy.\\n5. `analysis.md` \\u2014 macro table, per-task table, Wilcoxon tests, Bonferroni-corrected p's, macro-delta bootstrap 95% CIs, effect sizes, robustness re-runs.\\n6. `figures/` \\u2014 per-task accuracy bars, \\u0394 heatmap, macro-delta forest plot.\\n7. `DEVIATIONS.md` + run manifest with model ID, decode params, seeds.\\n\\n## 15. Concrete One-Page Run-Plan Reference (for the executing environment)\\n\\n```\\n1. git init experiment/\\n2. Place BBH JSONL files in data/bbh/<task>.jsonl; sha256sum > data/bbh.sha256\\n3. Place opro_per_task_instructions.csv in data/; sha256sum > data/opro.sha256\\n4. Export PREREGISTRATION.md\\n5. python src/build_prompts.py --tasks src/task_list.txt --conditions C0,C1,C2 \\\\\\n       --opro_csv data/opro_per_task_instructions.csv \\\\\\n       --out prompts/ --seed 42\\n6. python src/run_inference.py --prompts prompts/ \\\\\\n       --model <MODEL_ID> --temperature 0 --max_tokens_per_task src/max_tokens.csv \\\\\\n       --seeds 42,43,44 --out raw/\\n7. python src/score.py --predictions raw/ --gold data/bbh/ --tasks src/task_list.txt \\\\\\n       --out scores.jsonl\\n8. python src/analyze.py --scores scores.jsonl --out analysis.md --boot_n 10000 --seed 42\\n9. git add . && git commit -m \\\"run: <sha run_id>\\\"\\n```\\n\\n---\\n\\n**Closing note:** The repository supplied to this design contains only `task.json` and the literal pointer string `\\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\"` Every artifact in this design (BBH data, OPRO instructions, model checkpoints/APIs) must be obtained from public sources or external compute. No element of the design depends on hidden in-repo materials, and no part of this document claims that any experimental run has occurred in the current sandbox.\", \"evidence\": \"(1) Repository inspection is unchanged: /workspace contains exactly task.json (7 lines, 445 bytes); its documents.track.md is the literal string \\\"Design-only task; repositories and compute are not required.\\\" No source code, configs, scripts, READMEs, manifests, OPRO artifacts, or BBH artifacts are present. /home and ~ are empty; no project tree exists under /. find /workspace -maxdepth 3 -type f returned only that one file. (2) Environment inspection: nvidia-smi reports no NVIDIA driver (no GPU); curl https://www.google.com with -m 2 returned HTTP 000 (network disabled); system Python on Linux, 24 CPUs, no project dependencies installed; task.json states datasets: []. (3) Therefore all data, instructions, and model references in this design (BBH data from Suzgun et al. 2022 / lukaemon/bbh; OPRO per-task instructions from Yang et al. 2023 Appendix; PaLM 2-L model endpoint) trace to publicly available materials or to an external execution environment. (4) No experimental run has been performed in this sandbox; the deliverable above is a self-contained design-only document grounded in the locked defaults from the approved plan (per-task OPRO instructions; 21-task OPRO subset; PaLM 2-L; zero-shot; greedy decoding; answer-style cue bound to the instruction prefix; Bonferroni \\u03b1=0.025/contrast; paired Wilcoxon plus paired bootstrap CI). (5) Every item that would require a model call or external data is listed explicitly as such and tagged with the runnable step that would invoke it in an external environment.\", \"assumptions\": [\"Reference OPRO paper is Yang et al. 2023 Large Language Models as Optimizers; per-task optimized instructions are sourced verbatim from its Appendix and frozen as a CSV before any model call.\", \"Reference BBH is Suzgun et al. 2022 BIG-Bench Hard; the 21-task subset is defined by Yang et al. 2023 Appendix (single-string-answer subset of Suzgun's 23) and is read as the exact task list published with the paper rather than reconstructed.\", \"Eval split is the 250-example per-task BBH evaluation split, downloaded from lukaemon/bbh (HuggingFace) with hash-check against the original Suzgun release.\", \"Primary model is instruction-tuned PaLM 2-L via the OPRO paper's API; substitute models (text-bison-001, gpt-3.5-turbo, open-weights instruction-tuned models) are documented as parallel arms.\", \"Decoding is greedy at temperature 0.0 with top_p 1.0; the recorded score per cell is the macro-mean of 3 seeds if non-determinism is detected, otherwise the single deterministic pass.\", \"Few-shot = zero-shot; held constant across all three conditions.\", \"The answer-output cue (e.g., \\\"Answer with one of: yes, no.\\\") is bound to the instruction prefix so the empty cell carries no cue; this convention is fixed before unblinding.\", \"Scoring is exact-match after per-task normalization, defined ahead of time in this document; no LLM-as-judge is introduced.\", \"Inference is paired Wilcoxon signed-rank on per-task accuracy deltas with Bonferroni-corrected alpha = 0.025 per primary contrast, supplemented by a paired bootstrap 95% CI on macro-deltas; robustness re-runs include a length-matched random-instruction baseline and a 3-shot few-shot variant.\", \"Execution is infeasible in this bubblewrap (no GPU, network off, no model/API, no data) and is therefore deferred to an external compute environment; this design documents only the protocol and analysis plan.\"], \"uncertainties\": [\"Exact identity of the 21st-vs-23rd-and-27th BIG-Bench Hard task lists: this design defers to Yang et al. 2023 Appendix for the canonical 21-task list rather than re-enumerating here.\", \"Whether the hosted PaLM 2-L endpoint accepts a true greedy mode; if it does not, the design falls back to N=3 seeds and uses the median seed for the Wilcoxon test.\", \"Whether the BBH lukaemon/bbh release matches the original Suzgun files item-for-item; SHA-256 cross-check is in the run manifest but the experiment proceeds if the hashes agree on the per-task item text.\", \"Whether the published per-task OPRO instructions in Yang et al. 2023 contain any cases of implicit test-set overfitting; addressed only via reporting per-task \\u2206 and the falsification criterion in Section 1.\", \"Whether to add the universal OPRO instruction as a primary or robustness cell; default is robust-only (Section 4), not part of primary inference.\", \"Choice of post-correction method of multiple-testing across the 21 task-family McNemar tests; default is Benjamini-Hochberg FDR within the 21-task family (secondary inference only).\"]}", "origin": "completion"}, "llm_response_id": "chatcmpl-88459308-b123-4665-8962-0e70f3bb88ef", "security_risk": "UNKNOWN", "kind": "ActionEvent", "source_event_index": 23}
